# Keep the build context to what the bot actually runs.
#
# The Dockerfile does COPY . . , so anything here lands in the image. Before this
# file existed, 3.1 MB of .bak / .sync-conflict copies were being baked into every
# build. Mirrors .gitignore, with the additions that matter for an image.
#
# Unlike .gitignore, a pattern here matches from the context root only: "*.bak"
# excludes ./x.bak but not ./core/x.bak. Anything that can appear at any depth
# needs "**/". Without it 904 KB of __pycache__ - written by the running bot into
# its bind-mounted plugin directories - was being baked into every image.

# Ad-hoc backups - git history serves this purpose now
**/*.bak
**/*.bak-*
**/*.bak_*
**/*.sync-conflict-*
**/*.afterpatch-check

# Runtime state; these are bind-mounted at run time, not baked in.
# The settings template is the one exception: it's copied into an empty
# config folder on first start.
config/*
!config/.env.example
logs/
**/*.db
**/*.log

# Tests are run against a mounted source tree, not from inside the image
tests/
requirements-dev.txt

# Python and editor noise
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.DS_Store
.git/
.gitignore

# The website is built inside the image (see the Dockerfile's web stage)
web/node_modules/
web/cloudflare/node_modules/
web/dist/
web/dist-preview/
web/dist-project/
.impeccable/

# Repo-only material: README images, CI. None of it runs.
docs/
.github/
web/scripts/

# Local design notes (git-ignored too): never shipped in an image
PRODUCT.md

# Never committed or built into the image: settings, data, the app files an install hosts, keys.
.env
config/app/
config/cache/
**/*.db
**/*.db-*
**/*.keystore
**/*.jks
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
